WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:04.310
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:08.000
Today we’re going to talk about the material
point method.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:13.780
This method uses both grids and particles
to simulate the movement of snow, dripping

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:19.960
honey, interactions of granular solids and
a lot of other really cool phenomena on our

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:20.960
computers.

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:25.000
This can be used, for instance, in the movie
industry to simulate what a city would look

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.010
like if it were flooded.

00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:31.730
However, it has its own limitations, which
you will hear more about in a moment.

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:36.960
This paper showcases really cool improvements
to this technique, for instance, it enables

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:42.550
to run these simulations twice as fast, and
can simulate new phenomena that were previously

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:45.460
not supported by the material point method.

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:51.280
One is the simulation of complex thin boundaries
that enables us to cut things, so in this

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:54.760
video, expect lots of virtual characters to
get dismembered.

00:00:54.760 --> 00:01:00.440
I think this might be the only channel on
YouTube where we can say this celebrate it

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:02.970
as an amazing scientific discovery.

00:01:02.970 --> 00:01:08.180
And the other key improvement of this paper
is introducing two-way coupling, which means

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:13.080
the example that you see here as the water
changes the movement of the wheel, but the

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:16.810
wheel also changes with the movement of the
water.

00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:22.231
It is also demonstrated quite aptly here by
this elastoplastic jello scene, in which we

00:01:22.231 --> 00:01:26.659
can throw in a bunch of blocks of different
densities, and it is simulated beautifully

00:01:26.659 --> 00:01:31.760
here how they sink into the jello deeper and
deeper as a result.

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:41.310
Here, you see a real robot running around
in a granular medium, and here, we have a

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:46.960
simulation of the same phenomenon and can
marvel at how close the result is to what

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:49.280
would happen in real life.

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:54.400
Another selling point of this method is that
this is easy to implement, which is demonstrated

00:01:54.400 --> 00:02:00.690
here, and what you see here is the essence
of this algorithm implemented in 88 lines

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:02.620
of code.

00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:04.350
Wow!

00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:10.729
Now, these methods still take a while as there
is a lot of deformations and movement to compute

00:02:10.729 --> 00:02:16.419
and we can only advance time in very small
steps, and as a result, the speed of such

00:02:16.419 --> 00:02:22.029
simulations is still measured in not frames
per second, but in seconds per frame.

00:02:22.029 --> 00:02:26.370
These are the kinds of simulations that we
like to leave on the machine overnight.

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:30.819
If you want to see something that is done
with a remarkable amount of love and care,

00:02:30.819 --> 00:02:33.059
please read this paper.

00:02:33.059 --> 00:02:36.890
And I don’t know if you have heard about
this framework called Taichi.

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:41.669
This contains implementations for many amazing
papers in computer graphics.

00:02:41.669 --> 00:02:46.739
Lots of paper implementations on animation,
light transport simulations, you name it,

00:02:46.739 --> 00:02:50.620
a total of more than 40 papers are implemented
here.

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:56.739
And I was thinking, this is really amazing,
“I wonder which group made this”, then

00:02:56.739 --> 00:03:00.069
I noticed it was all written by one person.

00:03:00.069 --> 00:03:05.760
And that person is Yuanming Hu, the scientist
who is the lead author of this paper.

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:07.719
This is insanity.

00:03:07.719 --> 00:03:11.530
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

